    "collected_at": "2026-10-21T18:20:29.460825+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/new-york-short-line-wins-11-2-million-federal-rail-grant",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/new-york-short-line-wins-11-2-million-federal-rail-grant",
    "origin_publisher": "FreightWaves",
    "published_at": "2026-10-21T15:45:29+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-10-21T15:45:29+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-10-21T18:21:57.863128+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-10-21T18:21:57.863128+00:00",
    "body": "Ontario Midland Railroad has secured an $11.2 million federal grant to rehabilitate approximately 10 miles of track in Wayne County, New York, improving infrastructure that supports the region’s agricultural and manufacturing shippers.\nThe Wayne County Mainline Safety and Reliability Project will replace approximately nine miles of rail, resurface eight grade crossings, replace seven turnouts and remove three others. Work also includes ballast placement, tamping and track surfacing, according to an Oct. 19 announcement from the railroad and U.S. Sens. Chuck Schumer and Kirsten Gillibrand.\nThe award comes through the Federal Railroad Administration ’s Consolidated Rail Infrastructure and Safety Improvements, or CRISI, program. The project is intended to modernize deteriorating infrastructure, improve freight transportation efficiency and support economic development.\n“This funding will support comprehensive modernization of Ontario Midland Railroad’s east-west corridor by replacing aging infrastructure with new rail and crossties,” Wayne County Economic Development and Planning Executive Director Brian Pincelli said in the announcement.\nOntario Midland provides local freight service across Wayne and Monroe counties, including the eastern Rochester metropolitan area, and interchanges with CSX at Newark. The railroad has served the region since 1979.\n“We are profoundly grateful to Senators Schumer and Gillibrand for their relentless advocacy and partnership in securing this critical 2025–2026 grant for the Ontario Midland Railroad,” said Ontario Midland Chairman Robert Babcock and President Jeffrey Bauman, in a joint statement. “This federal support is a game-changer for our operations, ensuring we can maintain safe, reliable service and continue driving economic growth across the communities we proudly serve.”\nThe grant is part of a combined $20.8 million in CRISI funding announced for Ontario Midland and Falls Road Railroad. Falls Road received approximately $9.6 million to rehabilitate rail infrastructure along about 40 miles between Brockport and Lockport, including rail and tie replacement, bridge rehabilitation and resurfacing of six grade crossings.\nThe investments will help strengthen regional supply chains, reduce truck traffic and fuel consumption, and support businesses that depend on freight rail, the senators said.\nSubscribe to FreightWaves’ Rail e-newsletter and get the latest insights on rail freight right in your inbox.\nRead more articles by Stuart Chirls here .\nRead more:\nAutonomous railcar technology earns $16M federal grant\nUnion Pacific CFO to retire\nCSX eliminates more than 100 management positions\nIntermodal rail better by 10% in latest count CSX operations chief to retire\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
